Morretta et al. (2022) propose a formula where the average marginal cost of research (𝑀𝑃𝐶𝑟𝑒𝑠) is augmented by the average marginal cost of publishing (𝑀𝑃𝐶𝑝𝑢𝑏): 𝑀𝑃𝐶𝑡= 𝑀𝑃𝐶𝑟𝑒𝑠𝑡+ 𝑀𝑃𝐶𝑝𝑢𝑏𝑡= (𝑤𝑟𝑒𝑠𝑡∗ℎ𝑟𝑒𝑠𝑡
𝑦𝑟𝑒𝑠𝑡
) + (𝑤𝑝𝑢𝑏𝑡∗ℎ𝑝𝑢𝑏𝑡
𝑦𝑝𝑢𝑏𝑡
). They express the annual salary of authors in PPP for international comparisons (Rousseau et al., 2021).  Their productivity measure attempts to address co-authorship by considering full or partial credit attribution. Full attribution suffers from similar issues as the original equation. Partial credit divides credit uniformly among co-authors (Shen and Barabasi, 2014; Hirsch, 2007). For example, a paper with two authors assigns each 0.5 credit, and a paper with 100 authors assigns each 0.01. Partial credit always yields higher 𝑀𝑃𝐶 values than full credit. The second term considers the average wage of editors and reviewers, their time allocation, and the number of papers they handle.
